(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old man becomes ill over 4 days with a temperature of 40.1 °C (104.2 °F), shaking chills and confusion. He looks toxic, a new loud regurgitant murmur is heard, and painless hemorrhagic macules appear on his soles, which are Janeway lesions (painless, nontender spots). Which form of infective endocarditis fits his course?","choices":["Acute endocarditis","Subacute endocarditis","Libman-Sacks disease","Device endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute endocarditis is a hectically febrile illness from a virulent, invasive organism: rapid onset over 3-10 days, high fevers, systemic toxicity, rapid valve damage and seeding of extracardiac sites such as the skin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who started amlodipine for hypertension five weeks ago has bilateral, symmetric ankle swelling without warmth, redness or tenderness. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order a D-dimer","Review the medication as the cause","Bilateral leg ultrasound","Start therapeutic anticoagulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine commonly cause edema, typically bilateral and without signs of inflammation. Recognizing drug-induced edema and reviewing the medication list is the appropriate step. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which patient with recurrent pericarditis is described as a candidate for anti-interleukin-1 therapy?","choices":["One who is steroid-dependent","One in tamponade right now","One with a normal C-reactive protein","One with a first, mild episode"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent or colchicine-resistant disease, or steroid-dependent disease, with an inflammatory phenotype makes anakinra or rilonacept the preferred option. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with viridans streptococcal endocarditis had positive blood cultures on days 1 and 2 and her first negative culture on day 4. She asks how long her antibiotics will last. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["4-6 weeks counted from day 4","4-6 weeks from admission day","Until her fever settles","10 days from the first dose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Therapy is typically 4-6 weeks counted from the first negative culture, here day 4, because all bacteria in the vegetation must be killed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has three months of worsening breathlessness on exertion and ankle swelling. Dilated cardiomyopathy is suspected, but her 12-lead electrocardiogram is completely normal. Which interpretation of the normal tracing is most accurate?","choices":["It points to restrictive disease instead","It is what most dilated cardiomyopathy shows","It excludes every cause of heart failure","It makes dilated cardiomyopathy very unlikely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram is usually abnormal in dilated cardiomyopathy, showing atrial fibrillation, premature ventricular contractions or left bundle branch block, so a normal tracing almost rules the diagnosis out. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a diagnosed leg deep vein thrombosis, not yet treated, develops sudden shortness of breath and pleuritic chest pain. Which complication has occurred?","choices":["Pulmonary embolism","Post-thrombotic syndrome","Acute myocardial infarction","Septic thrombophlebitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Part of the thrombus can break off and travel to the lungs, causing a pulmonary embolism, the most important complication of deep vein thrombosis. Sudden breathlessness and pleuritic pain in this setting should prompt that concern. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man is newly diagnosed with restrictive cardiomyopathy caused by hemochromatosis, with iron deposited in the heart tissue. He has congestion and fatigue. Which is the first principle of his management?","choices":["Treat the underlying disorder","An inotrope to raise output","Septal myectomy for relief","A high-intensity exercise program"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of restrictive cardiomyopathy begins with treating the underlying disorder, such as iron storage in hemochromatosis, with cautious diuretics, rate-slowing drugs and anticoagulation added as needed. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has repeated dizziness and near-fainting while climbing stairs. His electrocardiogram shows the PR interval lengthening until a beat is dropped, in a repeating pattern. He takes no atrioventricular nodal blocking drugs, and no reversible cause is found. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Intravenous amiodarone","Synchronized cardioversion","Reassurance and follow-up"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type I needs no treatment unless symptoms are debilitating; for symptomatic patients with no reversible cause, a permanent pacemaker is considered. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has sudden severe pain in his right leg. Pulses are absent below the knee. Which examination finding is often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency?","choices":["Sensory loss over the foot's dorsum","Loss of hair on the shin","Complete paralysis of the leg","Absent patellar reflex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Light-touch sensory fibers are highly susceptible to ischemia, so sensory deficits over the dorsum of the foot are often the earliest neurologic sign of acute vascular insufficiency. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Idarucizumab is the reversal agent for which anticoagulant?","choices":["Warfarin","Unfractionated heparin","Dabigatran","Apixaban"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Idarucizumab reverses dabigatran in life-threatening or intracranial hemorrhage; each reversal agent is specific to its anticoagulant. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with recurrent pericarditis over several years now has leg edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. Constrictive pericarditis is suspected. Which echocardiogram finding would best support it?","choices":["A dilated, weakly contracting ventricle","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","Right ventricular diastolic collapse","Septal bounce toward the left ventricle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiographic features of constriction are increased pericardial thickness, abnormal septal motion or bounce toward the left ventricle, pronounced respiratory variation in ventricular filling and moderate biatrial enlargement. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"After a positive D-dimer in the low likelihood Wells category, which test comes next?","choices":["Contrast venography","A repeat D-dimer","Stopping the work-up","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A positive D-dimer is nonspecific, so it is followed by ultrasound of the leg; a positive ultrasound gives the diagnosis and a negative one rules it out. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man arrives with a temperature of 40.2 °C (104.4 °F), rigors and a new murmur, and he looks toxic. Acute endocarditis is suspected. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Two cultures within 1-2 hours, then antibiotics","Observation for 24 hours without treatment","Echocardiography before any cultures","Antibiotics first, cultures tomorrow"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If the presentation suggests acute endocarditis, two cultures are drawn within the first 1-2 hours, and empiric antibiotics are started only after the cultures are drawn. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 6-year-old with repaired congenital heart disease has 3 weeks of low-grade fever, fatigue, joint aches and weight loss. A new murmur is heard. Which organism most commonly causes endocarditis in this age group?","choices":["Enterococci","Candida species","Viridans group streptococci","Group B streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Children over 1 year with congenital heart disease develop indolent viridans group streptococcal disease; Staphylococcus aureus comes next. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which organism is a classic cause of acute infective endocarditis?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci","Streptococcus gallolyticus","Gram-negative coccobacilli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute infective endocarditis is caused by virulent organisms: beta-hemolytic streptococci and Staphylococcus aureus (methicillin-susceptible or methicillin-resistant), sometimes Staphylococcus lugdunensis, and enterococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man has known tachy-brady syndrome (a fast rhythm alternating with slow rates and pauses) and asks which fast rhythm is behind his palpitations. An ambulatory monitor is arranged. Which rhythm is it most likely to record during the fast episodes?","choices":["Junctional rhythm","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome the tachyarrhythmia that alternates with bradycardia or pauses is most commonly atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with acute pericarditis is starting ibuprofen and colchicine. He has a history of dyspepsia. Which addition to his regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["A prophylactic antibiotic","A proton pump inhibitor","An oral anticoagulant","A loop diuretic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal protection is provided with anti-inflammatory therapy, for example a proton pump inhibitor, because these drugs reduce the stomach's protective prostaglandins. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man receiving an unfractionated heparin infusion for deep vein thrombosis develops life-threatening bleeding. Which agent reverses the heparin?","choices":["Andexanet alfa","Protamine sulfate","Vitamin K by mouth","Idarucizumab"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Protamine sulfate is the reversal agent for unfractionated and low molecular weight heparin in life-threatening or intracranial hemorrhage; dabigatran, the factor Xa inhibitors and warfarin have other reversal options. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has an asymptomatic infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 3.4 cm on ultrasound. Which follow-up is most appropriate?","choices":["Imaging every 6 to 12 months","Ultrasound every three years","Elective repair now","No further imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Once identified, an aneurysm is followed for size and growth; a 3.0 to 3.9 cm aneurysm is imaged with ultrasound every three years. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man who lives at home has 3 weeks of low-grade fever and a new murmur. He has had no recent hospital stay, procedure or urinary symptoms, and no primary source of infection is found. Two separate blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis. How do these cultures count under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["A minor criterion","A major criterion","Excluded as contaminants","Not counted until echocardiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Community-acquired enterococci in the absence of a primary focus count as a typical organism, so growth from two separate blood cultures meets the major blood culture criterion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man who smokes and has diabetes and hypertension asks whether he is too young to have peripheral artery disease. Which age threshold applies to him?","choices":["Age 70 with risk factors","Age 60 regardless of risk","Age 50 with risk factors","Age 40 with a family history"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral artery disease is a concern from age 50 in people with risk factors such as smoking, diabetes and hypertension, and from over 60 in people without other risk factors. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has two weeks of exertional breathlessness and chest pressure. Her electrocardiogram shows a left bundle branch block; a tracing from last year was normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Evaluate for heart disease","Synchronized cardioversion","Reassure: a normal variant","Place a pacemaker now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A new left bundle branch block should always be considered a sign of pathology, so it is worked up for the underlying cardiac disease or injury rather than dismissed. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension has three months of progressive hoarseness. A chest radiograph shows widening of the mediastinal shadow. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Abdominal aortic aneurysm","Descending aortic aneurysm","Aortic arch aneurysm","Ascending aortic aneurysm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic aneurysm symptoms depend on location: an arch aneurysm causes hoarseness by involving the recurrent laryngeal nerve, and the chest film shows a widened mediastinum. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has had 20 minutes of sudden palpitations. Her heart rate is 188, blood pressure 122/76, and she is alert with no chest pain or breathlessness. The electrocardiogram shows a regular narrow complex tachycardia with no visible P waves. Which is the most appropriate first step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous procainamide","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A stable, regular narrow complex tachycardia above 160 with hidden P waves is supraventricular tachycardia, and the first-line treatment when stable is a vagal maneuver, then adenosine. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with hypertension and diabetes has nonvalvular atrial fibrillation and a high CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score. Which anticoagulant class is preferred?","choices":["Warfarin as first choice","Aspirin alone","No anticoagulant, given her age","A direct oral anticoagulant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Direct oral anticoagulants (dabigatran, apixaban, edoxaban or rivaroxaban) are preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation; a high stroke-risk score means she needs anticoagulation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man with 3 months of weight loss presents with dyspnea and a large pericardial effusion. He has had no recent viral illness, chest surgery or radiation. Which etiology should be most strongly suspected?","choices":["Rheumatic fever","Malignancy","Coxsackievirus","Dressler syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In older adults there is a higher index of suspicion for malignancy, especially with an effusion or tamponade, and his weight loss without a viral prodrome reinforces it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has scleroderma with Raynaud phenomenon, acid reflux, osteoporosis and long-standing pulmonary hypertension. Her electrocardiogram shows a right bundle branch block. Which of her conditions is a named cause of this block?","choices":["Raynaud phenomenon","Osteoporosis","Acid reflux","Pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary hypertension is on the list of common causes of right bundle branch block, alongside a sudden rise in right ventricular pressure from pulmonary embolism; her other conditions are not. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has typical acute viral pericarditis with stable vital signs and no high-risk features. A transthoracic echocardiogram is ordered. Which result is most expected?","choices":["A normal study or only a small effusion","Apical ballooning with a normal base","A thick pericardium with septal bounce","Right ventricular diastolic collapse"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The echocardiogram is often normal in acute pericarditis unless there is an associated effusion, and the chest radiograph is typically normal; the diagnosis is primarily clinical. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with chest pain and breathlessness after a recent viral illness is being evaluated for myocarditis. Her electrocardiogram shows sinus tachycardia. Which laboratory result would indicate cardiac myonecrosis?","choices":["A low natriuretic peptide","A low white cell count","Elevated serum troponin","Normal sedimentation rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Serum troponin is the indicator of cardiac myonecrosis in myocarditis. The rest of the work-up adds leukocytosis on the blood count, a possibly elevated sedimentation rate and electrocardiogram changes. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has just been diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy on echocardiography. He has two younger sisters and a brother. His mother had unexplained sudden death at 44. Which step for his family is most appropriate?","choices":["No family evaluation is needed","Evaluate only female relatives","Test relatives only if symptomatic","Evaluate his first-degree relatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is mostly inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, so one affected parent is enough to pass it on, and detection in a family member is a common reason evaluation of relatives begins. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has Mobitz type II block found on a routine tracing; she has no symptoms. Her family prefers to avoid procedures. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Referral to cardiology","Permanent pacemaker placement","Observation without pacing","Reviewing her medication list"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Observation alone should be avoided because Mobitz type II is considered dangerous even without symptoms; its progression is unpredictable, so the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Adenosine slows a tachycardia and reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White. Which drug is used?","choices":["A second dose of adenosine","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When slowing from adenosine reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, intravenous procainamide is used and adenosine is not repeated; adenosine and the other nodal blockers leave the accessory pathway unopposed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is 4 days into his recovery from a large anterior myocardial infarction when he suddenly becomes hypotensive, with distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Constrictive pericarditis","Dressler syndrome","Massive pulmonary embolism","Free-wall rupture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Free-wall rupture after myocardial infarction is a recognized cause of tamponade, alongside acute pericarditis, iatrogenic injury, trauma, malignancy and aortic dissection. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which electrical treatment is delivered for polymorphic ventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Unsynchronized defibrillation","Transcutaneous pacing","Drugs first, shock later"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia is never truly stable and its QRS shape keeps changing, so it is defibrillated (unsynchronized) even when a pulse is present. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman hospitalized after surgery has had a peripheral intravenous catheter in her arm for a week. She has a high fever, fluctuance over a tender vein, and redness spreading well past the vein. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Treat as infection within the vein","Compresses and ibuprofen with a recheck in 7 to 10 days","Start anticoagulation alone","Reassure that the process is self-limited"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High fever, fluctuance or purulence, and erythema extending far beyond the vein after cannulation indicate septic thrombophlebitis, which needs antimicrobial treatment and removal of the catheter rather than simple phlebitis care. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has enterococcal endocarditis of the aortic valve after recurrent urinary infections. Which outcome is he at notable risk for?","choices":["Septic lung emboli","Needing heart surgery","Spontaneous cure","Lowest death rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enterococcal endocarditis is difficult to treat because of antibiotic resistance, and major complications with need for valve replacement occur in about 30-40%. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman who is 34 weeks pregnant sits in a car for nine hours and develops left calf swelling and pain. Her pregnancy supplies a hypercoagulable state and the long ride supplies venous stasis. Which additional component would complete the Virchow triad?","choices":["Venous stasis","Arterial thrombosis","Endothelial trauma","Hypercoagulable state"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The Virchow triad is venous stasis, hypercoagulable state and endothelial trauma. Pregnancy and the car ride supply two components, so the missing one is injury to the vein wall, as from trauma, surgery, burns or injection. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with symptomatic varicose veins has worn stockings and exercised faithfully for several months with persistent aching. Duplex ultrasound confirms great saphenous vein reflux. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Lifelong observation only","Venous ablation therapy","Oral flavonoid therapy","Surgical valvuloplasty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A trial of conservative therapy with stockings is generally done for weeks to months before venous ablation is offered. She has finished that trial with persistent aching, and duplex confirms reflux; the goal is better symptoms and appearance. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 41, 43"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man started a new blood pressure medication two weeks ago. He now feels tired, and his pulse is 46 and regular, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Thyroid tests are normal. Which medication is the most likely cause?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Lisinopril","Metoprolol","Amlodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers such as metoprolol, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and digoxin are the drug causes of sinus bradycardia; a new one two weeks ago is the likely culprit. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has had an episode of fainting during basketball practice and brief runs of ventricular tachycardia on monitoring. She wants to keep playing on her competitive school team. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Unrestricted play with extra fluids","Activity restriction given her high risk","Clearance once the murmur softens","Competitive play on a beta blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Activity restriction for high-risk individuals is an adolescent strategy; her exertional syncope marks high risk of sudden death, which in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is highest in preadolescents and adolescents and related to extreme exertion. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slides 65, 66, 69, 76, 82, 105"},{"q":"What does the transthoracic echocardiogram show in Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Big atria, stiff normal ventricles","Septal wall of 1.5 cm or more","Four dilated, thin-walled chambers","Mid and apical wall hypokinesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The transthoracic echocardiogram shows wall-motion abnormality, hypokinesis or akinesis of the midsegment and apical segment of the left ventricle. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought in after a motorcycle crash with a large thigh wound and heavy blood loss. He has no pulse. The monitor shows a regular narrow-complex rhythm at 110 per minute. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Asystole","Sinus tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","Pulseless electrical activity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An organized rhythm on which a pulse would be expected, but with no pulse, is pulseless electrical activity; hypovolemia is one of its two leading causes in adults. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which aortic condition can also shed emboli that cause blue toe syndrome?","choices":["Pseudoaneurysm of the femoral","Aortic regurgitation","Carotid stenosis","Abdominal aortic aneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic, unruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm can produce embolic phenomena leading to blue toe syndrome, besides pain, early satiety and groin pain. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 70-year-old smoker has a painful, dry, punched-out ulcer at the tip of his second toe with minimal bleeding and surrounding black necrotic tissue. It started as a minor scrape months ago. Which complication is he at risk of?","choices":["Osteomyelitis","Aortic dissection","Venous insufficiency","Pseudoaneurysm formation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic skin ulcers fail to heal because blood supply cannot meet the demands of healing tissue; they sit at the most distal sites, are often ringed by dry gangrene, and can lead to osteomyelitis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy collapses briefly during a basketball game and recovers within a minute. Blood pressure and heart rate are normal. There is a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the left sternal border in the fourth intercostal space that does not radiate to the carotids; it grows louder when he stands and softer when he squats. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Acute myocarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exertional syncope in an adolescent with an outflow murmur at the left sternal border that does not reach the carotids, louder with standing and softer with squatting, is obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman who had a left leg deep vein thrombosis after a hip fracture ten years ago reports a heavy, aching left lower leg by evening that eases when she elevates it. Examination shows pitting edema, shiny taut skin at the ankle and brownish pigmentation. Pedal pulses are strong. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic venous insufficiency","Peripheral artery disease","Lymphedema","Acute deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Progressive pitting edema with dull discomfort worse on standing, shiny taut ankle skin and brownish pigmentation after a prior deep vein thrombosis are the classic picture of chronic venous insufficiency. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"When is the dental prophylaxis dose given?","choices":["The evening after","One week before","30 to 60 minutes before","Daily for a month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The single prophylactic dose is given 30-60 minutes before the procedure, so the drug is circulating when the transient bacteremia occurs. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has sudden, severe, tearing chest pain radiating to his back. Blood pressure is 184/106. Imaging confirms an aortic dissection. Which blood pressure goal applies?","choices":["Systolic above 140 mmHg","Systolic 100 to 120 mmHg","Systolic below 80 mmHg","No change until surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Management stops progression by reducing blood pressure, bringing systolic pressure to 100 to 120 mmHg with a beta blocker such as esmolol or a vasodilator such as nitroprusside. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has stable dilated cardiomyopathy of two years' duration with no systemic illness and no recent change in his heart function. He has read about heart biopsies online and asks whether he should have one. What is the role of a heart muscle biopsy for him?","choices":["Not routine and rarely useful","The gold standard for his disease","The first test before echocardiography","Required to confirm every case"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is not used routinely in dilated cardiomyopathy and is rarely useful. Its indications are a recent onset of rapidly deteriorating function or a systemic disease that can involve the heart. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man had his pacemaker generator changed 3 weeks ago. He now has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F), and imaging shows a vegetation on a pacemaker lead. Which organisms cause the largest share of this type of endocarditis?","choices":["Enterococci","Gram-negative coccobacilli","Viridans streptococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Device endocarditis is caused mostly by coagulase-negative staphylococci (about 41%) and Staphylococcus aureus (about 36%), and it can appear within weeks of implantation or a generator change. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman recovering well from her first episode of acute viral pericarditis asks how likely it is to come back. Which estimate is most accurate?","choices":["Under 1%","About 15 to 30%","About 60%","Over 90%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis affects about 15 to 30% of patients after acute pericarditis, which is why completing the full course and recognizing symptoms early matter. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which causes should be checked first in a patient with frequent premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Dehydration, fever","Hypoxemia, electrolytes","Hyperlipidemia, obesity","Hypertension, diabetes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxemia and electrolyte imbalances (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypercalcemia) are the main causes, so new frequent premature ventricular contractions call for checking oxygen and electrolytes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman on telemetry has torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). The first-line intravenous push broke the first run, but short runs keep recurring. She is alert with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Magnesium infusion","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Torsades in a conscious patient is broken with intravenous magnesium and then treated with more magnesium as an infusion: magnesium, and more magnesium. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with metastatic lung cancer has a symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion that has already reaccumulated once after drainage. Which consult is recommended to improve her outcomes and quality of life?","choices":["Physical therapy","Endocrinology","Palliative care","Rheumatology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptomatic malignant pericardial effusion carries a limited life expectancy (median 2 to 4 months) and often recurs; a palliative care consult is recommended to improve outcomes and quality of life. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has atrial flutter (sawtooth flutter waves) with a ventricular rate of 140. She is alert, with a blood pressure of 124/78 and no chest pain. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","A beta or calcium channel blocker","Adenosine twice","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically stable atrial flutter is rate-controlled with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker; rate control is the first goal, though harder to achieve than in fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a rhythm strip in which a normal beat is followed by an early beat with an abnormal P wave and a narrow QRS, repeating so that every other beat is early (bigeminy). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Junctional rhythm","Ventricular bigeminy","Flutter with 2:1 block","Atrial bigeminy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Early beats with an abnormal P wave and narrow QRS are premature atrial contractions (common in chronic lung disease), and an ectopic beat every other beat is bigeminy. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has recurrent atrioventricular nodal reentry episodes that tend to start after coffee, alcohol or decongestant (sympathomimetic) use. She has no heart disease. Which counseling is most appropriate?","choices":["Avoid exercise","Avoid her known triggers","Expect a pacemaker","Start an anticoagulant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nodal reentry may occur spontaneously or with exertion, caffeine, alcohol, beta-agonists and sympathomimetics, so limiting her known triggers is sensible counseling. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which medication is a recognized cause of pericardial effusion?","choices":["Metformin","Amlodipine","Levothyroxine","Doxorubicin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Medication causes of pericardial effusion include doxorubicin, minoxidil, phenytoin, and hydralazine and procainamide through drug-induced lupus. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What defines a transient ischemic attack?","choices":["Focal deficit lasting over 24 hours","Global confusion for one week","Headache without any deficit","Focal deficit lasting under 24 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A transient ischemic attack is a focal loss of neurologic function lasting less than 24 hours; it and stroke classify a patient with carotid disease as symptomatic. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with heart failure has runs of 5 identical wide QRS beats on a Holter monitor. He has no symptoms during them. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Start oral amiodarone","Synchronized cardioversion","Optimize his heart failure care","Implantable defibrillator now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia is managed by optimizing treatment of the underlying cardiac comorbidities; it is not by itself an indication for a defibrillator. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"}]};
